{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-126921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{3},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "881e375df23b4345500456650b2c1959ca0e140a48dc63ea2c8199d23ef16d8a"} {"question" "q-126921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}x \\\\ \\sqrt{3}y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126923" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "881e375df23b4345500456650b2c1959ca0e140a48dc63ea2c8199d23ef16d8a"} {"question" "q-126923" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126923" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126923" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "881e375df23b4345500456650b2c1959ca0e140a48dc63ea2c8199d23ef16d8a"} {"question" "q-126929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "15\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "881e375df23b4345500456650b2c1959ca0e140a48dc63ea2c8199d23ef16d8a"} {"question" "q-126930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-126930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-149683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-149683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=6,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-149683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "881e375df23b4345500456650b2c1959ca0e140a48dc63ea2c8199d23ef16d8a"} {"question" "q-149683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-149683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "6[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"} {"question" "q-149683" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061706/state.json" "file_sha256" "47c998146392a49c5e7ec74fc8b8c85e7cee9daad83ba16b4032782ecffb3b08"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1965 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
